                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <h3>What This Means for Travelers</h3>
            <p>Taking the train between Baltimore and Newark gives you a relatively short, laid‑back travel experience. It’s comparable to driving but allows you to relax, use Wi‑Fi, stretch your legs, and enjoy views of the Chesapeake Bay area, Philadelphia’s skyline, and the changing landscapes of New Jersey. Compared to road travel, the train avoids freeway traffic stress and delivers you straight into Newark’s downtown without needing a car.</p>
            <p>Traveling by train from Baltimore to Newark offers a relaxing alternative to driving or flying, especially if you value comfort, scenic views, and arrival in the city center. Even though the trip takes longer than a flight when considering airport procedures, it reduces stress from traffic and airport security. The route’s direct service makes it beginner‑friendly for first-time travelers.</p>
            <h3>Key Points:</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Perfect for day trips or weekend getaways if you plan your departure wisely.</li>
                <li>Traveling by train allows you to work, read, or simply enjoy the scenery along the Northeast Corridor.</li>
                <li>Consistent schedules ensure more predictable travel planning than variable road traffic.</li>
            </ul>
            <h2>Train Duration by Type</h2>
            <p>Amtrak runs the Baltimore → Newark route with multiple services, and there are typically two classes of service available on Northeast Regional and premium classes on Acela. While Acela is the fastest option in the Northeast Corridor, Northeast Regional and long-distance trains provide comfortable and steady alternatives.</p>

